WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.260
Welcome back to The Deep Dive. Today, we are really going under the hood, looking at some

00:05.260 --> 00:10.440
source material that I think fundamentally changes how you have to view your own mind.

00:10.540 --> 00:11.400
It really does.

00:11.480 --> 00:16.920
We're doing a deep dive into the very core of how we make decisions, and maybe more importantly,

00:17.440 --> 00:18.520
why we make mistakes.

00:18.720 --> 00:24.340
And that's such a crucial point, because for, I mean, for decades in neuroscience, whenever

00:24.340 --> 00:30.920
a researcher saw an error, you know, a wrong button press, a bad choice.

00:30.940 --> 00:31.640
Like a slip-up.

00:31.680 --> 00:32.900
A slip-up, exactly.

00:33.620 --> 00:37.020
It was always treated as a side effect.

00:37.200 --> 00:40.200
It was seen as a failure of the main system.

00:40.280 --> 00:41.220
It's noise, right?

00:41.220 --> 00:41.380
Right.

00:41.480 --> 00:45.860
It was either noise in the signal, or the brain didn't have enough evidence yet, or maybe it

00:45.860 --> 00:49.300
was some kind of failure of control that happened right at the last second.

00:49.300 --> 00:55.160
So the basic idea was, a successful brain is a computer running the right program.

00:55.740 --> 00:59.340
A brain that makes a mistake is a computer that just crashed for a moment.

00:59.660 --> 01:01.400
That's the classical view in a nutshell.

01:01.720 --> 01:06.300
But the sources we're digging into today, and this is some pretty groundbreaking stuff, using

01:06.300 --> 01:13.340
these incredible biomimetic models of the corticosteroidal system, and then validated with macaque data.

01:13.540 --> 01:16.300
Yeah, the Pathak et al. paper from 2025.

01:16.660 --> 01:17.040
That's the one.

01:17.040 --> 01:21.740
And it just suggests that this entire classical view is, well, fundamentally wrong.

01:22.120 --> 01:23.200
It's not just wrong.

01:23.300 --> 01:27.280
It presents a genuine crisis for how we think about decision-making.

01:27.840 --> 01:32.780
The research suggests errors aren't these sloppy, last-minute accidents.

01:33.040 --> 01:33.340
No.

01:33.780 --> 01:34.080
No.

01:34.240 --> 01:35.120
They're predictable.

01:35.880 --> 01:42.640
They are internally coherent, robust plans of action that the brain commits to right at

01:42.640 --> 01:44.200
the very beginning of the process.

01:44.340 --> 01:45.480
Okay, that changes everything.

01:45.480 --> 01:49.180
I mean, what does that do to our ideas about rationality or agency?

01:49.640 --> 01:55.340
If the choice is locked in before we're even aware of it, what does self-control even mean?

01:55.520 --> 01:57.700
That is the mission for this deep dive.

01:58.020 --> 02:04.660
We are going to unpack the discovery of these incredible cells, the incongruent neurons, or ICNs for short.

02:04.900 --> 02:05.420
ICNs.

02:05.680 --> 02:06.000
Got it.

02:06.000 --> 02:11.800
And then we'll get into the theoretical framework that this discovery supports, which is called the admissible histories approach.

02:12.080 --> 02:15.940
We really have to restructure how we think about time and choice in the brain.

02:16.060 --> 02:17.340
It's an ontological shift.

02:17.340 --> 02:28.860
And just to frame this whole conversation for you, get the staggering fact, the researchers found a group of neurons that can reliably predict that you're going to make a mistake.

02:28.860 --> 02:31.680
A full behavioral error.

02:32.560 --> 02:34.340
More than one second before you actually do it.

02:34.340 --> 02:35.280
Just think about that.

02:35.360 --> 02:36.500
A full second.

02:37.200 --> 02:41.500
These neurons fire off within the first 200 milliseconds of you seeing something.

02:41.900 --> 02:43.420
200 milliseconds is nothing.

02:43.680 --> 02:47.140
That's barely the time it takes for the light to hit your eye and get processed.

02:47.140 --> 02:47.620
Exactly.

02:48.380 --> 02:56.680
And yet, in that tiny window, the outcome of a complex choice that takes another one, two, maybe three seconds to unfold is already fixed.

02:57.260 --> 03:01.900
The mistake is already authorized and on its way before you've even had a chance to really think.

03:02.160 --> 03:03.140
A second of predetermination.

03:04.040 --> 03:04.460
Wow.

03:04.660 --> 03:05.580
That's wild.

03:05.840 --> 03:06.840
Okay, let's unpack this.

03:06.920 --> 03:07.100
Yeah.

03:07.380 --> 03:10.060
So how did they even find these ICNs?

03:10.100 --> 03:10.900
What was the breakthrough?

03:10.900 --> 03:20.900
Well, the initial discovery actually came from a computational model, a really sophisticated, biologically grounded model of these circuits called corticostreatal microassemblies.

03:21.780 --> 03:22.840
Okay, let's break that down.

03:23.380 --> 03:23.900
Corticostreatal.

03:23.960 --> 03:24.960
What are we talking about here?

03:25.320 --> 03:28.700
So think of it as the main highway for action in your brain.

03:28.700 --> 03:40.320
It's the communication link between your cortex, the outer thinking part, and the striatum, which is this deep structure that's all about action selection, habits, learning from rewards.

03:40.900 --> 03:47.160
So it's the circuit that turns a thought or a goal into an actual physical movement.

03:47.540 --> 03:48.140
Precisely.

03:48.580 --> 03:58.880
And by modeling the complex dynamics of how these cells talk to each other, the researchers could start to isolate different patterns of activity, different populations of neurons.

03:59.200 --> 04:01.960
And one of these populations was the ICNs.

04:02.380 --> 04:04.060
Why incongruent?

04:04.060 --> 04:11.060
They called them that because their firing pattern was really strong, really clear, and it was obviously driving an action.

04:11.540 --> 04:15.020
But that action was incongruous with the goal of the task.

04:15.160 --> 04:17.140
In other words, it was the wrong action.

04:17.460 --> 04:18.820
It led to no reward.

04:18.960 --> 04:19.480
Exactly.

04:19.820 --> 04:23.720
The action was perfectly formed, but externally it was a mistake.

04:24.260 --> 04:29.620
And this brings us back to that temporal paradox, which is the finding that just breaks all the old models.

04:29.620 --> 04:34.740
Let's walk through the sequence of events they saw in the macaques, step by step.

04:35.000 --> 04:35.240
Okay.

04:35.540 --> 04:37.240
So the stimulus appears on the screen.

04:38.300 --> 04:47.780
Then, within just 200 milliseconds, a fifth of a second, this population of ICNs associated with the incorrect choice fires.

04:48.180 --> 04:49.420
And it fires strongly.

04:49.700 --> 04:50.020
Wow.

04:50.020 --> 04:56.880
But the task itself, you know, gathering evidence, planning, and making the movement, that often takes a lot longer.

04:57.160 --> 05:00.120
Sometimes 1.2 seconds, sometimes two full seconds.

05:00.260 --> 05:06.020
And the actual mistake, the monkey pressing the wrong button, happens way at the end of that period.

05:06.080 --> 05:06.900
A huge delay.

05:07.280 --> 05:13.640
The neural commitment happens at 200 milliseconds, but the behavioral expression is over a second later.

05:13.840 --> 05:14.940
So this is the big question.

05:15.160 --> 05:15.480
Mm-hmm.

05:15.480 --> 05:19.500
What on earth is happening in that 1,000 millisecond gap?

05:19.620 --> 05:19.960
Mm-hmm.

05:20.060 --> 05:25.360
Because if the outcome is already locked in, the brain can't be doing what we always thought it was doing,

05:25.480 --> 05:30.880
which is, you know, carefully weighing the evidence, deliberating, still making up its mind.

05:30.940 --> 05:32.200
It's not deliberating.

05:32.340 --> 05:33.300
That's the key insult.

05:33.400 --> 05:34.300
It's unfolding.

05:34.860 --> 05:35.300
Unfold.

05:35.380 --> 05:36.820
Think of it like a lint fuse.

05:36.820 --> 05:41.780
The ICN activity at 200 milliseconds isn't a prediction that a mistake might happen.

05:42.020 --> 05:44.300
It's the signal that the fuse has been lit.

05:44.300 --> 05:47.780
The system has entered a state where the outcome is already determined.

05:48.400 --> 05:55.780
So the rest of that time, that full second, is just the time it takes for the fuse to burn down to the firecracker.

05:55.920 --> 05:56.940
That's a great analogy.

05:57.600 --> 06:04.960
It's just the necessary biological time it takes for that cascade of neural signals to travel through all the motor pathways

06:04.960 --> 06:07.780
and physically express itself as a movement.

06:08.280 --> 06:09.660
The decision is done.

06:09.860 --> 06:11.780
The rest is just execution.

06:11.780 --> 06:14.400
And the sources are really clear on this point.

06:14.900 --> 06:18.160
These ICNs aren't just some random flicker of noise.

06:18.860 --> 06:22.840
Because the old models would just say, oh, that's just some ambiguous early activity.

06:23.140 --> 06:23.220
Right.

06:23.340 --> 06:24.260
They dismiss it.

06:24.600 --> 06:27.760
But the ICN signal is incredibly robust.

06:28.080 --> 06:34.140
It has these key properties that make it functionally identical to the neurons that drive the correct response.

06:34.320 --> 06:34.560
Okay.

06:34.620 --> 06:35.560
What are those properties?

06:35.560 --> 06:36.960
Well, first, they're stable.

06:37.160 --> 06:40.300
They show up reliably from one trial to the next in the same context.

06:40.540 --> 06:42.400
Second, they're highly selective.

06:42.940 --> 06:44.980
An ICN doesn't just fire randomly.

06:45.460 --> 06:50.260
It fires for a specific stimulus leading to a specific incorrect answer.

06:50.460 --> 06:53.000
Ah, so it's not just a general oops signal.

06:53.460 --> 06:56.620
It's a signal for do this specific wrong thing.

06:56.960 --> 06:57.420
Exactly.

06:58.020 --> 06:59.360
Third, it's causal.

06:59.940 --> 07:01.640
They've shown it's upstream of the error.

07:02.120 --> 07:04.360
It helps drive the physical mistake.

07:04.920 --> 07:07.500
And fourth, and this is maybe the most important part.

07:07.600 --> 07:08.340
The robustness.

07:08.480 --> 07:09.360
The robustness.

07:09.540 --> 07:10.620
The level of activity.

07:10.840 --> 07:18.520
The firing rate of an ICN that leads to a mistake is just as strong as the firing rate of a correct neuron that leads to a reward.

07:18.520 --> 07:22.460
So from the brain's internal point of view, it's not making an error.

07:22.640 --> 07:23.260
Not at all.

07:23.380 --> 07:30.260
From inside the system, this is a perfectly legitimate, well-formed, high-confidence action plan.

07:31.160 --> 07:40.320
The only thing that labels it an error is the outside world, the rules of the experiment, which says, sorry, no reward for that one.

07:40.440 --> 07:47.500
This is forcing us to make a really sharp distinction then between two separate moments in time, commitment and expression.

07:47.500 --> 07:48.820
That's the core distinction.

07:49.320 --> 07:51.020
Commitment is that point of no return.

07:51.340 --> 07:59.380
It's the moment the internal dynamics of the brain push the neural state into a valley where there's only one way out, only one possible outcome.

08:00.020 --> 08:02.560
The ICNs are the flag that marks that moment.

08:02.920 --> 08:07.560
And expression is just the final act, the observable behavior that happens much later.

08:08.180 --> 08:16.160
The ICNs are the smoking gun showing that for mistakes, commitment happens way, way before expression.

08:16.160 --> 08:18.980
And, you know, this idea does have some history.

08:19.140 --> 08:22.620
People might remember the famous Libet experiments from the 80s.

08:22.680 --> 08:22.800
Right.

08:23.140 --> 08:24.580
The readiness potential.

08:24.820 --> 08:25.380
Exactly.

08:26.020 --> 08:33.940
Libet showed that general brain activity predicting a movement happens before you're consciously aware of your intention to move.

08:34.080 --> 08:34.920
But this is different.

08:35.080 --> 08:36.480
This feels much more specific.

08:36.900 --> 08:39.520
It's way more specific and far more predictive.

08:39.860 --> 08:42.100
Libet was about voluntary action in general.

08:42.100 --> 08:48.820
The ICN finding applies this logic to a specific, evaluated outcome, an error.

08:49.140 --> 08:53.560
And it predicts the specific, incorrect choice with near certainty.

08:54.040 --> 08:56.980
So Libet showed activity comes before general intention.

08:57.560 --> 09:03.880
This shows that a specific commitment to a mistake is made, deterministically, a full second before it happens.

09:03.880 --> 09:07.520
It's like we're watching the brain commit to a plan in slow motion.

09:08.200 --> 09:10.700
Once those ICNs fire, the verdict is in.

09:10.920 --> 09:15.120
The rest is just the system waiting for the clock to run out before the action takes place.

09:15.380 --> 09:26.420
Okay, so that deep dive into what ICNs are and what they do, it makes it painfully obvious why the old models of decision making just can't cope with this.

09:26.500 --> 09:27.420
They really can't.

09:27.420 --> 09:28.880
Let's talk about why.

09:29.760 --> 09:36.840
Why does this classical framework, which has been the dominant way of thinking for so long, just fall apart here?

09:37.080 --> 09:40.500
Well, we can call that whole framework the state-first ontology.

09:40.500 --> 09:51.400
And it's all built around the idea that a decision is a single computation happening either instantly or gradually over time that can be described by a single evolving state.

09:51.640 --> 09:56.300
And errors in that view are just a failure to compute the right state.

09:56.300 --> 09:57.860
A failure to compute, exactly.

09:58.080 --> 10:05.840
Probably the most famous and, I mean, honestly, the most successful model in all of cognitive science is the drift-diffusion model, the DDM.

10:06.240 --> 10:08.820
Can you give us a quick refresher on how that works?

10:08.920 --> 10:09.220
Sure.

10:09.740 --> 10:12.080
The DDM is beautiful in its simplicity.

10:12.640 --> 10:17.580
It imagines a decision as a process of accumulating evidence for one of two choices.

10:18.260 --> 10:21.920
Think of a little particle drifting between two lines or two thresholds.

10:21.920 --> 10:23.760
One for correct and one for incorrect.

10:23.760 --> 10:29.340
Exactly. And the evidence you get from the world pushes the particle toward the correct line.

10:29.720 --> 10:32.000
But there's always some noise, so it jitters around.

10:32.280 --> 10:35.220
Whichever line it hits first, that's the decision you make.

10:35.700 --> 10:41.060
And time in that model is just how long it takes for the particle to finally hit a threshold.

10:41.320 --> 10:45.300
Right. And it explains the speed-accuracy trade-off perfectly.

10:45.500 --> 10:50.600
If you lower the thresholds, you decide faster, but you're more likely to hit the wrong one by accident.

10:50.600 --> 10:52.080
It's incredibly elegant.

10:52.300 --> 10:55.440
But the ICN finding creates a huge problem for it.

10:55.640 --> 10:58.520
A fatal incompatibility, you could say.

10:58.640 --> 11:04.100
It creates a temporal dilemma that the DDM just can't solve without basically making itself useless.

11:04.320 --> 11:05.620
Okay, lay it out for us.

11:05.640 --> 11:09.960
We've got this idea of a gradual drift of evidence on one hand,

11:10.140 --> 11:14.720
and then we've got this decisive neural commitment at 200 milliseconds on the other.

11:14.720 --> 11:20.400
And you can't have both. So if you try to map the ICN activity onto the DDM, you hit a wall.

11:21.200 --> 11:22.340
Here's the first problem.

11:22.640 --> 11:22.800
Oh.

11:23.080 --> 11:30.200
If the decision variable, that drifting particle, is still somewhere in the middle at 200 milliseconds,

11:30.480 --> 11:33.200
which it should be in any slow task.

11:33.300 --> 11:34.860
Yeah, it's still accumulating evidence.

11:34.860 --> 11:37.920
Then any neural activity at that point should be ambiguous.

11:38.180 --> 11:39.820
It should be weak or noisy.

11:40.380 --> 11:43.440
But the ICN signal isn't weak or noisy.

11:43.620 --> 11:46.120
It's powerful and highly predictive.

11:46.560 --> 11:46.720
Okay.

11:47.120 --> 11:49.840
So what's the other horn of the dilemma?

11:50.300 --> 11:52.480
The other problem is, what if you say,

11:52.880 --> 11:58.080
okay, maybe the model is wrong and the threshold was already crossed at 200 milliseconds?

11:58.600 --> 12:01.860
To explain the certainty of the ICN signal.

12:01.940 --> 12:02.100
Right.

12:02.100 --> 12:06.300
Well, if that's true, then what is the brain doing for the next full second?

12:06.660 --> 12:10.340
That entire stretch of processing time becomes completely meaningless.

12:10.680 --> 12:11.960
It becomes epiphenomenal.

12:12.420 --> 12:13.500
A ghost in the machine.

12:13.600 --> 12:14.040
Exactly.

12:14.460 --> 12:16.020
If the decision is already made,

12:16.200 --> 12:19.240
then the BDM has to include the system as just sitting there,

12:19.480 --> 12:20.980
twiddling its thumbs for a second.

12:21.340 --> 12:24.120
And that collapses the entire purpose of the model,

12:24.260 --> 12:29.060
which is to describe this gradual, time-consuming process of deliberation.

12:29.060 --> 12:32.460
Now, I can hear someone thinking, well, couldn't you just tweak the model?

12:33.460 --> 12:40.160
What if the ICNs just represent some kind of early bias and the accumulation continues after that?

12:40.520 --> 12:43.940
People have tried, but the problem goes deeper than just the timing.

12:44.200 --> 12:47.560
It's a problem of language, of representational vocabulary.

12:47.560 --> 12:48.640
What do you mean by that?

12:48.980 --> 12:53.620
The DDM describes how a single value estate evolves over time,

12:54.040 --> 13:00.260
but it has no way to describe the commitment to a whole complex, time-extended sequence of future states.

13:00.480 --> 13:03.260
It can't talk about a pre-authorized trajectory.

13:03.500 --> 13:05.640
So it can describe the rate of change right now,

13:05.960 --> 13:09.800
but it can't capture the commitment to the entire shape of the path ahead.

13:09.800 --> 13:10.560
You've got it.

13:10.800 --> 13:15.440
That representational vacuum means the DBM, while it's still useful for some things,

13:15.840 --> 13:20.560
just fails to capture the core neural reality that this new research has uncovered.

13:20.920 --> 13:24.920
What about other big models, like the Bayesian or inference-based models?

13:25.020 --> 13:26.220
They seem a bit more sophisticated.

13:26.900 --> 13:30.420
They are, but they run into a very similar problem with time.

13:30.820 --> 13:34.700
In a Bayesian world, you make errors because you don't have enough evidence,

13:34.880 --> 13:38.780
or because you have a really strong, incorrect prior belief.

13:38.780 --> 13:40.900
Your internal assumption is just wrong.

13:41.100 --> 13:41.360
Right.

13:41.660 --> 13:45.420
Now, for the system to commit to an error in just 200 milliseconds,

13:46.240 --> 13:49.300
that Bayesian explanation has to become really extreme.

13:49.480 --> 13:49.920
How so?

13:50.140 --> 13:55.560
It would have to mean that your internal prior belief was so overwhelmingly powerful

13:55.560 --> 13:59.540
that it just instantly steamrolled any sensory evidence coming in.

13:59.860 --> 14:05.180
Or that the brain just made a snap judgment based on a tiny, compressed piece of information

14:05.180 --> 14:07.900
and then ignored everything else that came after.

14:07.900 --> 14:08.460
Right.

14:09.100 --> 14:14.180
But even if you accept that, you're still stuck with the same problem as the DDM.

14:15.140 --> 14:17.500
Why does the trial keep going for another second?

14:17.920 --> 14:21.960
If the inference is complete, why is the system still processing?

14:22.460 --> 14:24.760
And this is where the new framework has an answer.

14:25.120 --> 14:26.520
It has a powerful answer.

14:27.020 --> 14:31.680
The old models all assume that the future is something you simulate or predict.

14:31.680 --> 14:36.680
The new framework says, no, the future is something that is ontologically realized.

14:36.780 --> 14:38.180
It's not a hypothetical path.

14:38.280 --> 14:41.640
It's a real physical path the brain has already started walking down.

14:42.040 --> 14:42.480
Exactly.

14:42.940 --> 14:48.080
The choice to walk down that path has been made, and the ICN is the signpost at the entrance.

14:48.480 --> 14:51.940
So this isn't just about tweaking a parameter in an old model.

14:52.340 --> 14:55.040
This is a signal that we need a whole new way of thinking.

14:55.040 --> 15:00.760
We have to stop seeing time as just a clock that measures how long a decision takes.

15:00.760 --> 15:04.380
I start seeing time as the very fabric of the decision itself.

15:04.580 --> 15:07.460
The decision is the entire trajectory through time.

15:07.540 --> 15:09.280
Which brings us to this new way of thinking.

15:09.420 --> 15:09.620
Yeah.

15:09.620 --> 15:11.780
The trajectory-first ontology.

15:12.140 --> 15:15.780
That's the paradigm shift that this research is forcing on us.

15:16.500 --> 15:20.280
We have to change our fundamental unit of analysis.

15:20.880 --> 15:27.780
It's no longer the instantaneous neural state or some abstract value of accumulated evidence.

15:28.040 --> 15:30.580
It's the whole movie, not just a single frame.

15:30.760 --> 15:31.280
Exactly.

15:31.520 --> 15:34.800
The fundamental unit is the temporally extended history.

15:35.080 --> 15:35.320
Okay.

15:35.540 --> 15:37.800
Let's be really clear for everyone listening.

15:37.800 --> 15:41.200
What do you mean by a history with a capital H?

15:41.540 --> 15:49.660
A history, which the paper calls dollar, is just the complete ordered sequence of neural states from the very beginning,

15:49.940 --> 15:55.220
from the stimulus popping up on the screen, all the way to the very end, the physical action being completed.

15:55.440 --> 15:58.100
So it's the entire neural journey for a single decision.

15:58.300 --> 15:59.140
The entire journey.

15:59.320 --> 16:05.580
And the power of this framework is that it lets us make a really clean separation between two different concepts.

16:05.580 --> 16:08.180
A separation that the old models couldn't make.

16:08.360 --> 16:08.880
And that is?

16:08.960 --> 16:12.320
The separation between what the system can do and what the system should do.

16:12.520 --> 16:15.340
Internal possibility versus external value.

16:15.600 --> 16:16.200
Precisely.

16:16.660 --> 16:18.680
We start with internal coherence.

16:19.340 --> 16:23.640
This is the set of all admissible histories, which we can call dollar.

16:23.640 --> 16:35.820
A history is admissible if the brain's wiring its architecture, its synaptic weights, its dynamics, can actually produce that sequence of states.

16:35.820 --> 16:37.700
So this is just a physics question.

16:38.460 --> 16:40.320
Can the machine do this, yes or no?

16:40.440 --> 16:43.980
It has nothing to do with whether it's a good or bad action.

16:44.060 --> 16:44.960
Nothing at all.

16:45.020 --> 16:46.120
It's purely structural.

16:46.740 --> 16:54.880
So if you've trained your brain to pick option A 90% of the time, the path to pick option B is still an admissible history.

16:55.240 --> 16:57.440
The wiring for it still exists.

16:57.660 --> 17:00.500
It's still possible for the system to go down that path.

17:00.580 --> 17:01.880
Okay, so that's admissibility.

17:02.000 --> 17:02.720
What's the other half?

17:02.720 --> 17:06.080
The other half is external valuation or valuation ballers.

17:06.240 --> 17:07.960
And this is a sign by the outside world.

17:08.420 --> 17:13.420
This is the part where the experimenter says that action gets a reward or that action gets you nothing.

17:13.860 --> 17:17.040
This is where the labels correct and incorrect finally come into play.

17:17.280 --> 17:17.660
Exactly.

17:18.140 --> 17:22.500
And this separation immediately solves the puzzle of the ICNs.

17:22.840 --> 17:24.960
It lets us have error without failure.

17:25.460 --> 17:29.880
The ICNs represent trajectories that are perfectly admissible.

17:29.880 --> 17:32.040
They are part of the set dollars.

17:32.040 --> 17:34.000
They are computationally coherent.

17:34.200 --> 17:35.720
They're dynamically robust.

17:36.340 --> 17:39.700
The system is not failing when it executes them.

17:40.340 --> 17:41.920
But they get a negative valuation.

17:42.240 --> 17:43.600
They're punched by the outside world.

17:43.720 --> 17:44.040
Right.

17:44.540 --> 17:46.980
They are not failures of the system's mechanics.

17:47.520 --> 17:52.440
They are simply selections of an alternative future that turns out to be a bad one.

17:52.440 --> 17:55.540
So the ICN isn't encoding error.

17:56.120 --> 18:01.760
It's encoding a perfectly good action plan that just happens to be the wrong one for the current rules.

18:02.140 --> 18:05.900
It's like having a perfectly functional car with a GPS that can take you anywhere.

18:06.340 --> 18:07.500
The car never breaks down.

18:07.700 --> 18:08.560
That's the admissibility.

18:08.560 --> 18:11.220
But if you type in the wrong address...

18:11.220 --> 18:12.800
The car will drive you there perfectly.

18:13.220 --> 18:17.240
The mechanics were flawless, but the valuation of your destination was poor.

18:17.840 --> 18:23.340
The ICN is like the GPS confidently saying, now turning left towards the wrong address.

18:23.440 --> 18:24.360
That's a great analogy.

18:24.900 --> 18:30.700
It really decouples the internal success of the computation from the external success of the outcome.

18:30.980 --> 18:32.520
It's a profound move.

18:32.520 --> 18:37.320
Now, let's connect this back to the timing, to that 200 millisecond commitment.

18:37.880 --> 18:41.320
The sources talk about a concept called prefix closure.

18:41.780 --> 18:45.280
That sounds a little jargony, so let's really break that down.

18:45.340 --> 18:47.160
It sounds more complicated than it is.

18:47.380 --> 18:51.680
Prefix closure is a really simple but powerful idea about sequences.

18:52.320 --> 18:56.160
It just means that if a whole complete history is possible...

18:56.160 --> 18:58.920
So the whole trip from start to finish is admissible.

18:58.920 --> 19:02.620
Then every initial part of that trip must also be admissible.

19:02.820 --> 19:06.940
You can't magically get to the end of the journey without having completed the beginning parts of it.

19:07.220 --> 19:07.340
Right.

19:07.620 --> 19:13.000
If making a cake is an admissible history, then the first step, mixing the flour and sugar,

19:13.320 --> 19:16.060
has to be an admissible prefix of that history.

19:16.240 --> 19:16.660
Exactly.

19:16.980 --> 19:23.460
And the crucial insight here is that the ICN activity, that flash of firing at 200 milliseconds,

19:24.240 --> 19:26.040
represents a decisive prefix.

19:26.280 --> 19:27.540
A decisive prefix.

19:27.540 --> 19:28.060
Yes.

19:28.620 --> 19:32.520
The brain's activity has entered an initial state, a particular pattern,

19:32.800 --> 19:35.740
that is only a prefix for one possible future.

19:36.200 --> 19:40.700
It's structurally connected only to the sequence of states that leads to the error.

19:41.040 --> 19:42.920
So it's like entering a one-way street.

19:43.320 --> 19:46.300
The moment you make that turn at the 200 millisecond mark,

19:46.460 --> 19:49.200
the rest of your path is completely determined.

19:49.580 --> 19:52.560
Even if it takes you five more minutes to get to the end of the road,

19:53.160 --> 19:55.920
that turn is the decisive prefix.

19:55.920 --> 19:57.260
That's it, precisely.

19:57.720 --> 20:03.500
Once the system enters that ICN marked prefix, all the other possible futures collapse.

20:04.060 --> 20:10.560
The multiplicity of what could happen just disappears, and you're locked onto a single predetermined branch.

20:10.560 --> 20:15.560
The probability of making a mistake, given you've entered that prefix, shoots up to basically 100%.

20:16.080 --> 20:16.660
Pretty much.

20:17.060 --> 20:20.920
And so that long one-second delay suddenly makes sense again.

20:21.120 --> 20:22.940
The brain isn't sitting around deliberating.

20:23.080 --> 20:23.740
It's busy.

20:24.280 --> 20:29.360
It's executing all the necessary sequential steps of the history it has already committed to.

20:29.360 --> 20:32.480
And that just happens to take a second of biological time.

20:32.820 --> 20:36.800
Okay, but this raises a really obvious and, I think, critical question.

20:37.280 --> 20:44.060
If these error trajectories, these dollar histories, are always punished, why doesn't the brain just get rid of them?

20:44.360 --> 20:48.940
Why don't we learn our way to perfection and eliminate the ICNs and the paths they represent?

20:49.240 --> 20:49.580
Right.

20:49.580 --> 20:54.860
If the brain is this amazing learning machine, why can't it just delete the bad code?

20:54.960 --> 21:00.200
Why don't we ever reach 100% accuracy, even on a task we've practiced a thousand times?

21:00.980 --> 21:07.440
And the answer comes down to the physical, biological constraints of how learning actually works.

21:07.600 --> 21:13.820
We often think of the brain as this ideal optimization machine, but it's a physical object.

21:13.820 --> 21:18.960
And biological learning, like reinforcement learning, it's an incremental process, right?

21:19.300 --> 21:19.440
Yeah.

21:19.440 --> 21:22.660
It's not like a programmer going in and deleting a line of code.

21:22.840 --> 21:24.140
It's not deletion at all.

21:24.260 --> 21:26.560
It's all about adjusting synaptic weights.

21:27.300 --> 21:33.280
When a pathway leads to a reward, the connections along that path get a little bit stronger.

21:33.640 --> 21:36.900
When a path leads to nothing, it gets a little bit weaker.

21:36.940 --> 21:38.420
That's amplification and suppression.

21:38.580 --> 21:40.080
It's amplification and suppression.

21:40.080 --> 21:45.220
So the correct pathways, the dollar pluses issues, they get amplified and become these

21:45.220 --> 21:49.500
huge, wide superhighways that are overwhelmingly likely to be taken.

21:49.740 --> 21:52.900
But the alternative paths, the dollar histories...

21:52.900 --> 21:55.340
They just become these tiny, narrow back roads.

21:55.520 --> 21:55.780
Yeah.

21:56.020 --> 21:56.840
But they're still there.

21:57.140 --> 21:58.080
They're still on the map.

21:58.640 --> 22:04.860
As long as those synaptic weights are not absolute zero, and they never are, the pathways

22:04.860 --> 22:06.760
remain dynamically available.

22:06.760 --> 22:11.100
They exist as latent possibilities within the structure.

22:11.440 --> 22:13.120
And this isn't a bug in the system.

22:13.260 --> 22:15.140
You're saying this is actually a feature.

22:15.520 --> 22:16.720
It's a necessary feature.

22:17.020 --> 22:20.260
And it's driven by the very nature of our brain's architecture.

22:20.860 --> 22:22.040
I mean, think about the cortex.

22:22.300 --> 22:24.120
It's a massively competitive environment.

22:24.520 --> 22:27.400
You have neurons constantly trying to inhibit their neighbors.

22:27.600 --> 22:28.280
All the time.

22:28.280 --> 22:34.540
It's called lateral inhibition, and it's how the brain forces a clear winner-take-all outcome

22:34.540 --> 22:38.040
instead of just having a murky average of activity.

22:38.540 --> 22:43.740
So in that competition, even a tiny bit of random noise at the beginning could be enough

22:43.740 --> 22:44.720
to tip the scales.

22:45.100 --> 22:49.760
It could be just enough to give the ICN-associated pathway an early advantage.

22:49.920 --> 22:54.800
And once it wins that initial competition, the whole trajectory is set in motion.

22:54.800 --> 22:59.360
Those ICNs are the neural embodiment of those latent alternative futures.

22:59.560 --> 23:00.960
The paths that could be taken.

23:01.340 --> 23:04.740
The paths that could be taken, but which are usually suppressed.

23:05.380 --> 23:11.060
And the reason they are never fully erased by learning is because erasing them would come

23:11.060 --> 23:12.900
at a catastrophic cost.

23:13.020 --> 23:14.460
And that cost is flexibility.

23:14.780 --> 23:15.840
That's the cost.

23:16.180 --> 23:17.440
This is so important.

23:17.440 --> 23:23.780
The persistence of these error-prone histories is not a failure of learning.

23:24.100 --> 23:28.560
It is the structural necessity that allows us to be adaptable.

23:28.800 --> 23:29.020
Okay.

23:29.320 --> 23:29.900
Explain that.

23:30.220 --> 23:34.740
How does the ability to make a mistake help us adapt?

23:35.020 --> 23:38.180
Imagine a brain that had optimized itself to perfection.

23:38.580 --> 23:42.840
It has deleted every single pathway except the one that leads to the current reward.

23:43.260 --> 23:44.700
It's 100% accurate.

23:44.840 --> 23:45.420
Sounds pretty good.

23:45.420 --> 23:46.500
It sounds great.

23:46.620 --> 23:48.180
Until the rules of the world change.

23:48.740 --> 23:52.220
Suddenly, the reward is switched from option A to option B.

23:52.820 --> 23:55.480
What does our perfect, brittle system do?

23:55.580 --> 23:56.400
It's stuck.

23:56.860 --> 23:58.180
It has no pathway to B.

23:58.300 --> 24:01.720
It would have to slowly and painfully build a whole new road from scratch.

24:01.840 --> 24:02.280
Exactly.

24:02.420 --> 24:03.520
It's incredibly inefficient.

24:03.840 --> 24:08.180
But our actual brains, which have kept those other pathways around, they're just suppressed

24:08.180 --> 24:11.000
and narrow, can adapt much faster.

24:11.000 --> 24:15.520
All it has to do is start strengthening an existing road, not build a new one.

24:15.840 --> 24:22.140
So the capacity for error is the physiological price we pay for being able to learn quickly

24:22.140 --> 24:24.120
and survive in a changing world.

24:24.380 --> 24:25.720
That is the essence of it.

24:25.720 --> 24:30.920
The reason your error rate never truly hits zero is because those mistakes are the system

24:30.920 --> 24:34.600
occasionally taking a valid but disfavored path.

24:34.780 --> 24:41.340
And keeping those paths open is essential for rapid reconfiguration when the world throws you a curveball.

24:41.340 --> 24:47.400
So the mistakes we make are actually a sign that our brains are still flexible, still ready to learn something new.

24:47.640 --> 24:54.400
You can't have perfect adaptability without retaining the capacity to sometimes enter a perfectly admissible

24:54.400 --> 24:56.780
but currently incorrect trajectory.

24:57.380 --> 25:00.340
It's the cost of doing business for a biological brain.

25:01.000 --> 25:04.020
Okay, this is where things get really, really interesting for me.

25:04.100 --> 25:04.660
The intervention.

25:04.660 --> 25:06.100
The intervention experiment.

25:06.240 --> 25:11.740
Because it's one thing to show a correlation to say, hey, these ICNs fire before a mistake.

25:12.260 --> 25:15.540
But it's another thing entirely to prove causation.

25:15.740 --> 25:20.860
And this is the empirical high point of the research, where they actually manipulate the system in real time.

25:21.200 --> 25:28.240
So they took this incredible insight that the die is cast at 200 milliseconds, and they decided to intervene.

25:29.020 --> 25:29.940
How did they do it?

25:29.940 --> 25:33.700
So they didn't just sit back and record the ICN activity.

25:34.140 --> 25:42.560
They built a system that could detect that specific neural signature, that decisive prefix, the moment it happened.

25:42.680 --> 25:44.600
Within that first 200 milliseconds.

25:44.820 --> 25:45.800
In real time.

25:46.000 --> 25:50.360
And as soon as they detected it, they immediately halted the trial that has stopped everything

25:50.360 --> 25:53.440
before the monkey could actually make the incorrect movement.

25:53.440 --> 26:01.420
So the commitment to the error has happened inside the brain, but the external expression of the error is prevented.

26:02.000 --> 26:05.060
It's like they cut the trajectory off midstream.

26:05.220 --> 26:05.740
What happened?

26:06.040 --> 26:10.980
The result was a significant improvement in the overall accuracy for the entire session.

26:11.620 --> 26:17.460
By catching and stopping the error trajectories before they could complete, the net performance of the system went up.

26:17.540 --> 26:21.520
And this result just completely torpedoes the classical models.

26:21.520 --> 26:26.660
It does. Because in an old model like the DDM, what would this intervention be doing?

26:26.780 --> 26:30.960
You're just stopping a trial where the evidence happens to be drifting the wrong way.

26:31.040 --> 26:34.060
It shouldn't fundamentally change the system's overall behavior.

26:34.340 --> 26:36.580
It's not error correction. That's the key.

26:36.640 --> 26:39.060
It can't be. There was no error to correct yet.

26:39.280 --> 26:41.680
The monkey never actually pressed the wrong button.

26:42.500 --> 26:46.120
The intervention is purely perspective, not reactive.

26:46.120 --> 26:54.420
It's proof that the future was already determined, because by erasing that specific future, they improved the system's present performance.

26:54.580 --> 26:59.360
And that means the intervention wasn't acting on a momentary, ambiguous state.

26:59.500 --> 27:01.380
It was acting on a whole trajectory.

27:02.440 --> 27:07.400
By stopping the trial, what they were really doing was defining a pruned set of histories.

27:07.740 --> 27:09.760
Crooning the tree of possibilities.

27:09.760 --> 27:18.280
They were systematically snipping off the branches that led to the negatively valued outcomes, the dollar histories, before they could fully grow.

27:18.840 --> 27:24.820
And what's really crucial is that this didn't change the internal dynamics of the correct histories at all.

27:25.060 --> 27:28.280
The brain didn't magically get better at the task.

27:28.740 --> 27:33.520
They just externally prevented it from executing its occasional admissible mistakes.

27:33.520 --> 27:37.460
And that is the slam-dunk causal evidence.

27:38.100 --> 27:43.700
It proves that these error trajectories are not just some statistical pattern we see after the fact.

27:44.040 --> 27:51.700
They are fully specified and causally real things inside the brain, with momentum, long before any behavior happens.

27:51.980 --> 27:56.280
This has to completely change how we think about the idea of cognitive control.

27:56.280 --> 28:01.380
Completely. We've always had this idea of control as, you know, an internal brake pedal.

28:01.540 --> 28:05.300
A little supervisor in your head that catches a mistake at the last second and says,

28:05.760 --> 28:06.800
Whoa, stop.

28:07.000 --> 28:08.300
A last-ditch correction.

28:08.540 --> 28:09.840
A last-ditch effort.

28:10.160 --> 28:15.160
But what this research shows is that, by that point, it's almost always too late.

28:15.480 --> 28:17.860
The system is past the decisive prefix.

28:18.160 --> 28:20.760
It's already in irreversible execution mode.

28:20.860 --> 28:24.000
So real cognitive control isn't about hitting the brakes.

28:24.000 --> 28:25.920
It's about something that happens much earlier.

28:26.260 --> 28:30.340
It's the proactive, early suppression of disfavored trajectories.

28:30.580 --> 28:33.960
It's about pruning the future before it even starts to unfold.

28:34.440 --> 28:37.800
This reframes the whole idea of self-control in our daily lives.

28:38.160 --> 28:42.100
We think of it as this epic battle wrestling with the temptation in the moment.

28:42.480 --> 28:48.320
Right. If you're trying not to eat a cookie, we think self-control is the fight your hand is having on the way to your mouth.

28:48.800 --> 28:50.280
But that's the expression phase.

28:50.740 --> 28:52.440
The commitment was made long before.

28:52.440 --> 29:00.440
True self-control was what happened or failed to happen in that first 200 milliseconds when the thought cookie first popped into your head.

29:00.580 --> 29:01.000
Exactly.

29:01.600 --> 29:05.180
It's about whether you let that initial prefix gain momentum.

29:06.120 --> 29:09.620
A failure of self-control is a failure of early pruning.

29:10.360 --> 29:15.780
It's allowing a coherent, admissible, but ultimately bad trajectory to get going.

29:16.080 --> 29:20.860
So the intervention experiment is like an externalized form of perfect self-control.

29:20.860 --> 29:22.600
That's a perfect way to put it.

29:22.740 --> 29:30.200
It shows that if we could somehow internally replicate that process veto, that decisive prefix, before the clock runs down,

29:30.860 --> 29:37.800
we could achieve incredible accuracy, even while the structural possibility for error still exists within us.

29:37.920 --> 29:40.420
Okay, let's pull the camera back and look at the big picture.

29:40.620 --> 29:45.560
We've got this time gap, this trajectory first idea, the causal proof from pruning.

29:45.560 --> 29:50.600
What does this do to the big philosophical ideas that we use to understand ourselves?

29:51.140 --> 29:52.280
Let's start with agency.

29:52.780 --> 29:55.780
Agency takes a big hit, at least in its traditional form.

29:56.260 --> 30:05.300
If the decision is effectively fixed a full second before the action, you can't really locate your free will or your choice in that final moment.

30:05.300 --> 30:09.520
So my feeling of choosing to press the button right now is sort of an illusion.

30:10.220 --> 30:17.380
The decisive selection happened much earlier, and it was heavily constrained by the structure and history of your brain.

30:17.820 --> 30:22.020
So agency in this new framework has to be something different.

30:22.400 --> 30:23.260
What does it become?

30:23.460 --> 30:28.600
It becomes the capacity to shape the space of possible futures over the long term.

30:28.600 --> 30:37.140
It's the ongoing work of learning, of practicing, of paying attention, all the things that slowly change the underlying probabilities.

30:37.540 --> 30:41.100
It's about pruning some paths and widening others.

30:41.320 --> 30:43.640
So agency isn't a single event in time.

30:44.120 --> 30:50.020
It's the slow, continuous process of being an architect for your own brain's possibility space.

30:50.140 --> 30:50.560
Exactly.

30:50.820 --> 30:55.280
It's less about the choice and more about structuring the potential for future choices.

30:55.540 --> 30:55.840
Wow.

30:56.160 --> 30:57.540
Okay, what about rationality?

30:57.540 --> 31:02.940
We tend to think of being rational as, you know, computing the best answer.

31:03.120 --> 31:03.240
Right.

31:03.440 --> 31:05.880
Maximizing your utility, getting the most reward.

31:06.120 --> 31:08.480
But this framework seems to suggest something else.

31:08.660 --> 31:09.260
It does.

31:09.720 --> 31:17.660
It suggests that rationality isn't an active computation as much as it is an emergent property of successful suppression.

31:18.180 --> 31:19.020
What do you mean by that?

31:19.020 --> 31:30.240
You are rational not because you calculated the single best path, but because you successfully avoided entering the thousands of other perfectly possible but suboptimal paths.

31:30.240 --> 31:32.840
So rationality is defined by what you don't do.

31:32.840 --> 31:36.020
It becomes a fundamentally negative definition.

31:36.740 --> 31:41.300
A rational person is someone whose brain is very, very good at early pruning.

31:41.740 --> 31:47.300
If you manage your money well, your rationality isn't just in the one good investment you made.

31:47.500 --> 31:55.460
It's in the hundreds of terrible, impulsive spending trajectories that you successfully suppressed before they ever got started.

31:55.460 --> 31:57.960
That is a stunning way to reframe it.

31:58.480 --> 32:01.520
Success is defined by the failures you manage to avoid.

32:01.680 --> 32:03.680
It really turns the definition on its head.

32:03.920 --> 32:08.340
Now, how does all of this connect with other big complex theories out there?

32:08.700 --> 32:10.660
The one that comes to mind is active inference.

32:11.040 --> 32:12.120
Kari Friston's work.

32:12.460 --> 32:12.740
Yeah.

32:12.940 --> 32:14.760
That's a really important connection to make.

32:14.960 --> 32:17.100
Active inference is hugely influential.

32:17.100 --> 32:25.100
This idea that the brain is always trying to minimize surprise or free energy by making inferences about the future.

32:25.420 --> 32:28.820
And it talks about policies, which are future action sequences.

32:29.120 --> 32:30.100
It's already temporal.

32:30.720 --> 32:33.900
Why doesn't it fully capture this ICN finding?

32:34.660 --> 32:40.320
Well, active inference is forward-looking, but it often still comes from a state-first perspective.

32:40.320 --> 32:46.880
It views those future policies as hypothetical things that are simulated and weighed from your current state.

32:47.500 --> 32:49.140
The key difference is ontological.

32:49.860 --> 32:52.100
In AI, the future is inferred.

32:52.560 --> 32:54.400
And in the admissible history framework?

32:54.820 --> 32:56.380
The future history is realized.

32:56.760 --> 33:00.060
It's a physically possible path encoded in the brain's wiring.

33:00.620 --> 33:06.280
The admissible history framework basically provides the structural foundation on which active inference has to operate.

33:06.280 --> 33:07.640
Ah, okay.

33:08.060 --> 33:15.220
So the set of admissible histories, dollar, tells you which policies or trajectories even exist to be considered in the first place.

33:15.680 --> 33:16.160
Exactly.

33:16.160 --> 33:19.740
Sawade defines the set of physically possible futures.

33:20.420 --> 33:30.860
And then active inference comes in and provides a beautiful mathematical story for how the brain might weigh and prune those available futures to select the best one.

33:31.260 --> 33:32.800
They actually work together beautifully.

33:33.480 --> 33:37.240
This really solidifies the idea that we need a new unit of cognition.

33:37.920 --> 33:42.000
We have to stop looking at the snapshot, at the instantaneous neural state.

33:42.000 --> 33:45.700
The true unit of analysis has to be the authorized trajectory.

33:46.260 --> 33:47.440
The entire history.

33:47.820 --> 33:50.920
The ICNs are so important because they show us that boundary line.

33:51.180 --> 33:56.160
The point where a mere prediction about the future turns into an irreversible commitment to a future.

33:56.680 --> 33:58.640
The brain doesn't just guess what might happen.

33:58.640 --> 34:01.520
It enters a path that ensures what will happen.

34:02.540 --> 34:04.420
Hashtag tagged tag hag outro.

34:04.420 --> 34:09.800
So after this deep dive, we've really moved our whole understanding of what a mistake is.

34:10.000 --> 34:13.160
It's not just a computational glitch, a bit of noise in the machine.

34:13.380 --> 34:13.960
Not at all.

34:14.240 --> 34:24.380
We land on this really radical idea that an error is the successful execution of a legitimate, internally coherent plan that the outside world just happens to penalize.

34:24.380 --> 34:26.220
The brain doesn't stumble into error.

34:26.220 --> 34:32.180
It commits to it early, confidently, and with full dynamic certainty.

34:32.540 --> 34:37.000
Which means accuracy getting things right isn't about running the correct calculation.

34:37.300 --> 34:45.060
It's about the constant, active, and successful suppression of all the other admissible but incorrect trajectories.

34:45.280 --> 34:48.160
So let's leave our listeners with a final provocative thought.

34:48.160 --> 34:57.380
If the capacity to make mistakes, to have these dollar histories, is a structural necessity for a flexible, adaptable mind,

34:57.580 --> 35:03.160
and if our commitments to these mistakes are made so far in advance of our conscious awareness,

35:04.000 --> 35:07.540
then where can we find our real leverage for control?

35:08.160 --> 35:15.580
It suggests that the most profound form of control isn't found in that last-second, white-knuckled struggle with temptation.

35:15.580 --> 35:18.200
That moment is almost always too late.

35:18.300 --> 35:18.840
So where is it?

35:19.300 --> 35:23.420
The highest form of self-control might be something much quieter happening in the background.

35:23.900 --> 35:27.420
It's the slow, patient work of cultivating your own neural architecture,

35:27.680 --> 35:30.880
adjusting the dynamics, the biases, the learning rates,

35:31.080 --> 35:36.700
in a way that physically prunes the set of bad but possible futures you can even enter.

35:36.700 --> 35:40.820
You control your future, not by fighting it in the moment,

35:41.360 --> 35:46.080
but by sculpting the landscape of your own mind long before the choice ever appears.

